flooring, vinyl, and engineered/composite rigid core vinyl; porcelain and ceramic tile, porcelain mosaics and tile slabs; grout, mortar, backer board, tools, adhesives, underlayments, moldings, and stair treads; and decorative tiles and mosaics, such as natural stone, porcelain, ceramic, glass, wall tile, and decorative trims. The company also provides solid prefinished and unfinished hardwood, engineered hardwood, bamboo, and wood countertops; marble, limestone, travertine, slate, ledger; and vanities, shower doors, bath accessories, faucets, sinks, custom countertops, bathroom mirrors, bathroom lighting, and kitchen cabinets. It sells products through its warehouse-format stores and five small-format design studios, as well as through Website, FloorandDecor.com. The company serves professional installers, commercial businesses, and homeowners. The company was formerly known as FDO Holdings, Inc. and changed its name to Floor & Decor Holdings, Inc. in April 2017. Floor & Decor Holdings, Inc. was founded in 2000 and is headquartered in Atlanta, Georgia.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Bradley S. Paulsen | President, CEO & Director | Age 49 | Compensation: $2.7M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 10K people. Revenue per employee of $447K ranks in the above the sector median among Consumer Cyclical peers, with revenue growing 16%/yr across its filed history. Each employee generates $6K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $3K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 6\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags low compensation governance risk (3/10).","financial_summary":["Floor & Decor Holdings, Inc. is a mid-cap consumer cyclical business with a $5.0B market cap on $4.7B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +3% over the past three years, which puts it in mature, slow-growth territory.","Returns on capital run at 12% against a 11% cost of capital, which means the business barely earns its keep economically, with gross margins sitting at 42%.","The business throws off $64M in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 1 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 1.83\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","On the balance sheet, leverage is comfortably conservative at 0.4\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, and interest coverage of 79\u00d7 is fortress-like.","Shareholder yield is only 0.0%, a thin return of capital, and insider ownership is negligible, so management has little personal stake in the long-term outcome."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Price sits 126% above estimated fair value \u2014 no margin of safety left, and the cushion against estimate error has been bid away (confirmed by 1 other valuation measure)","sev":"red","cat":"valuation","magnitude":1.2596282450231615},{"text":"Fragmented industry with below-average profit capture \u2014 no moat to protect margins","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Altman Z in the grey zone \u2014 financial health warrants monitoring","sev":"amber","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Elevated short interest (14.9% of float) \u2014 sophisticated money is betting the other way","sev":"amber","cat":"sentiment"},{"text":"Heavy insider selling (net $15.1M)","sev":"amber","cat":"insider","dollars":-15068311.7},{"text":"Consumer demand is fundamentally cyclical \u2014 a recession compresses both unit volume and pricing power, and operating leverage cuts both ways.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"E-commerce margin compression and tariff exposure \u2014 digital competition erodes pricing power while trade policy reshuffles supply-chain cost structures overnight.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Brand relevance decay \u2014 shifting consumer preferences and social-media-driven trends can strand inventory and invalidate positioning faster than product cycles can adapt.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesi